WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.444
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.253 --> 00:00:08.170
KAVITHA GOPALAKRISHNAN:
Before we get started,

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:10.740
let's take a quick look at
the evolution of the cloud

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:11.700
landscape.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.960
I know that this has
been the buzzword,

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.379
so to speak, since the
last several years now.

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:19.530
If you look here, you'll
see that as we started

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:22.290
this migration to the
cloud, it started out

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:24.310
in a bunch of different flavors.

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:26.880
So there were combinations
of hybrid cloud,

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:28.470
which is part on-premise.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:31.170
And then part of your workload's
residing in the cloud.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:32.880
Then there were
also public cloud.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.050
There was private cloud.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.233
Of course, due to security
and other reasons,

00:00:36.233 --> 00:00:37.900
there were a lot of
enterprise companies

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:39.990
that preferred starting
out their cloud

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:43.540
journey with the private cloud
instead of a public cloud.

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:47.790
But what we can see here is
that over the last three years,

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:49.920
of all of these different
combinations that we just

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.290
talked about, the public
cloud is the only one

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:53.550
that's been growing.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:56.490
So we've been seeing an
increasing trend of enterprises

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:59.830
wanting to move more and more
of their workloads to the cloud.

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:02.710
We'll be talking about some
of the benefits, of course.

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:04.620
But in general, at
a high level, this

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.140
is, of course, because
of the flexibility

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.690
that a public cloud
brings to the fore.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:12.660
So you don't really have to
worry about the management

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:13.348
aspect of it.

00:01:13.348 --> 00:01:15.390
You don't have to worry
about things like scaling

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:18.360
up, scaling down, failover,
disaster recovery, things

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:19.230
like that.

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:22.230
And as we evolve, we expect
to see more enterprises

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:25.050
wanting to leverage these
capabilities that are all

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.750
provided by public clouds.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:30.930
If we take another
step in this direction,

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:34.310
again, we're looking at
the industry trends here.

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:37.380
You'll see that the
top couple of reasons

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:40.470
or the top couple of projects
that enterprises are really

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:44.160
investing in are optimizing
their existing cloud use.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:47.100
So this is companies
that have already

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:49.840
invested in the public
cloud to some extent,

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.050
but they just want
to optimize more.

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:55.830
Again, this may be for cost
savings or other motivations

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:56.710
as well.

00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:58.290
And then there
are companies that

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:00.660
are starting to move more
and more of their workloads

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:01.400
to the cloud.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:08.690
Databases, of course, are very
central to cloud migrations.

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:11.940
But as you know, they're
also really tough.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:14.630
And this is due to a number
of different reasons.

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:16.850
So we're going to take
apart maybe a couple

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:18.750
of the top reasons.

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:20.540
The first one is,
of course, there's

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:23.240
a lot of proprietary
technology and functionality

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:27.380
that's built into a lot of the
legacy database technologies.

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.350
So if we talk about Oracle--

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:31.720
I'm just using that as a
frame of reference here--

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:33.830
that may be things
like stored procedure,

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:37.140
custom functions, Oracle
RAC, so on and so forth.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:38.960
So this makes it
really hard for you

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:42.690
to migrate to an equivalent
cloud-native technology.

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:47.090
The second one is, of course,
your historical on-prem

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:49.090
databases are all
pretty monolithic.

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:52.610
So these are humongous
servers which don't really

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:55.040
fit into the cloud way
of doing things, right?

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:56.960
So oftentimes,
again, if you take

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:58.520
this huge, monolithic
server, you

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:02.730
have to split it up into
multiple servers in the cloud.

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:04.980
And the third thing,
which a lot of companies

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.130
actually maybe underestimate,
is the reliance

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:09.930
of applications on databases.

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:13.410
So if you think about a
company that's been around for,

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:15.990
let's say, 10, 15
years, as your business

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:18.630
grows, you have
hundreds or thousands

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:22.180
of apps, both internal- and
external-facing applications,

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:25.200
that are all relying
on the database.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.090
So as you're thinking
about migration,

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:30.360
it's really important for you
to consider the risks that

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:32.260
are associated as
you're migrating these

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:35.340
to different cloud
technologies and obviously

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:40.460
trying to minimize your
exposure as you do this.

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:42.470
So because of all of
these complexities

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:45.620
that we talked about,
we see two general types

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:47.300
of database migrations.

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:49.860
So there's a
homogeneous migration,

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:53.450
which is also commonly
known as lift and shift,

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:56.330
where you're retaining
the same database engine,

00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:59.930
but you're just moving over
from on-premise to the cloud.

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:03.630
While this does bring some
benefits, like, for example,

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:06.170
you already have
a trained-up staff

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:09.540
that understands the technology,
that's really familiar with it.

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:12.710
And so there's not as
much of a learning curve

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:14.450
associated with it
as you're migrating

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:16.430
the stack over to the cloud.

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:21.829
So that, by itself, extends into
faster migration time as well.

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:24.560
And like I said, in this case,
because the database engine is

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:27.530
still the same, you have
fewer application rewrites,

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:29.430
which, of course, means
that, in some cases,

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:31.130
you might have lower risk.

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:33.500
Now, that being said, there
are also some challenges

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:36.380
that we briefly talked about
in the previous slide, which

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:38.840
is server sizing
and configuration.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:42.500
Like I said, if you're talking
about huge, monolithic servers,

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:45.230
moving this to an
equivalent way in the cloud

00:04:45.230 --> 00:04:46.740
is going to be really tough.

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:48.240
So you have to
think about how you

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:51.500
can split up this huge server
into multiple different

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:53.010
servers.

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:56.330
The other type of migration is
called heterogeneous migration.

00:04:56.330 --> 00:05:00.070
People commonly also use
this in the same vein

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:03.530
as modernization or
digital transformation,

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:06.400
which is essentially saying
that, OK, I want to port

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:07.540
over my database engine.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.790
I've already invested a
lot in these licenses.

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:12.250
And the cost of
maintaining those licenses

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:14.650
is actually going up
with every passing year.

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:16.570
And as my business
grows, I'm actually

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:19.850
building a lot more
applications on top of that.

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:21.460
So what that means
is that you're

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:23.710
saying that, OK, I'm going
to make a business decision

00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:25.510
to invest more in the cloud.

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:27.460
And I'm going to do
what I need to do

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:31.270
to actually make it fit into
the cloud way of doing things.

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:33.310
Now, some of the
benefits for this--

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:37.770
we commonly see cost
savings, or lowering our TCO

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:40.210
is one huge thing that we see.

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:44.470
The second thing is also
reducing your reliance

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:47.950
on a particular technology.

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:50.710
As we've seen the
evolution of public cloud,

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:53.860
there are more and more
open-source-based technologies.

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:56.650
So we're going to talk about
Postgres today and a little bit

00:05:56.650 --> 00:05:58.340
about MySQL as well.

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:00.415
And we see this across
the database spectrum--

00:06:00.415 --> 00:06:01.790
not just in the
relational world,

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:04.160
but a lot in the
non-relational world as well.

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:06.197
So by moving to
these technologies,

00:06:06.197 --> 00:06:07.780
you're actually
lowering your reliance

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:10.408
on a particular
vendor, as well as

00:06:10.408 --> 00:06:11.950
the proprietary
technology that comes

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:13.870
from that particular vendor.

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:16.390
And then, of course,
because you're in the cloud,

00:06:16.390 --> 00:06:20.620
it makes it a lot easier for you
to scale up and down as needed

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:23.780
so that you don't have to have
reserved capacity at all times.

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:28.240
So if you're a retailer who's
thinking about peak season

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:31.060
times, like Black Friday
sales, or Christmas holidays,

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:32.770
or something like
that, you don't really

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:36.280
need to hold on to that capacity
for the rest of the year.

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:37.990
You can scale up
and down as needed.

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:40.760
And you don't really
have to worry about that.

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:43.810
The other thing is, of course,
failover and disaster recovery.

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:46.730
Instead of investing
in your own data center

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:48.730
and maintaining them
all around the year,

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:52.000
you can actually offload
that to the cloud provider.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.620
And as we move into the
cloud way of architecting

00:06:56.620 --> 00:06:58.900
our applications, of
course, more and more

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:01.970
companies want to just take
care of the innovation piece--

00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:05.740
the things that truly help
you derive business insight

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:09.250
and value instead of
things like, how do I

00:07:09.250 --> 00:07:10.610
manage my infrastructure?

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:11.990
How do I manage my database?

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:12.990
How do I configure this?

00:07:12.990 --> 00:07:14.948
They don't really want
to get into the business

00:07:14.948 --> 00:07:15.550
of doing that.

00:07:15.550 --> 00:07:17.920
And they would rather
have the public cloud

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:20.830
provider take care of that.

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:22.570
Of course, some
challenges with this--

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:25.180
it's going to be a
more complex, probably

00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:28.460
longer migration effort.

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:32.290
And when you're moving from
one database technology

00:07:32.290 --> 00:07:35.380
to another one, you also
have to be mindful of things

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:37.280
like schema conversion.

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:38.770
Another thing that
we talked about

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:41.710
was, especially if you're
talking about production grid

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:45.430
applications, you have to make
sure that there's no data loss.

00:07:45.430 --> 00:07:47.710
So that's where your
real-time change data capture

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:50.090
comes into play,
making sure that you

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:53.090
are able to migrate
your data in real time

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:55.150
instead of doing it in batches.

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:58.060
So these are the challenges
that we typically see.

00:08:00.950 --> 00:08:02.810
So now, I'll hand
it over to David

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:05.570
to take us through the key
steps that are involved

00:08:05.570 --> 00:08:07.110
in any database migration.

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:08.840
And then we'll get
into the details,

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:12.020
specifically from migrating
your databases from Oracle

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:12.784
to Postgres.

00:08:12.784 --> 00:08:14.201
DAVID YAHALOM:
Thank you, Kavitha.

00:08:14.201 --> 00:08:16.190
KAVITHA GOPALAKRISHNAN: Yeah.

00:08:16.190 --> 00:08:20.330
DAVID YAHALOM: So every
database migration project

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:22.340
is complicated,
specifically when

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:24.362
it comes to heterogeneous
database migrations,

00:08:24.362 --> 00:08:25.820
because as Kavitha
mentioned, there

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:27.470
are challenges which
we can overcome,

00:08:27.470 --> 00:08:29.110
but there are challenges.

00:08:29.110 --> 00:08:31.520
And what you see here is kind
of like taking a database

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:34.130
migration project and
breaking it down to,

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:35.782
I would say, the
six primary steps.

00:08:35.782 --> 00:08:37.740
And we're going to go
through each one of them,

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:40.640
talk about some of
the, I would say,

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:42.860
areas that you should
be made aware of,

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:46.010
some of the tips and
recommendations we can share.

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:47.480
From our experience,
as I said, we

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:50.240
have done countless
heterogeneous database

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:51.890
migrations, both
on-prem and off-prem,

00:08:51.890 --> 00:08:54.410
and of course, obviously,
on-prem to the cloud.

00:08:54.410 --> 00:08:57.420
So we would love to share
this experience with you.

00:08:57.420 --> 00:09:00.230
So let's go over the steps,
starting with assessment

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:02.000
and source discovery.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:04.850
So this is extremely
important, because it's

00:09:04.850 --> 00:09:08.270
the first step in your
database migration journey.

00:09:08.270 --> 00:09:10.280
You need to start
by understanding

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:13.990
what you currently have in
your existing environment.

00:09:13.990 --> 00:09:16.340
Specifically, this
talk is about Oracle

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:19.520
to Postgres migrations, the
Cloud SQL Postgres migration.

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:23.060
But this is also applicable
when you change database engines

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:25.850
from other source and
target technologies.

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:29.780
Understanding the source
environment, what you currently

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:33.980
have running right now,
the layout and structure

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:36.230
of your Oracle databases
before you even

00:09:36.230 --> 00:09:39.210
start the migration
might seem like trivial.

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:40.490
But it's super important.

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:43.430
And that's because a lot
of the features which

00:09:43.430 --> 00:09:45.680
you may be using right
now in your Oracle

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:48.740
database, your
high-availability configuration,

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:51.380
you may be using Oracle
React or Data Guard.

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:53.960
You might be using some
proprietary features.

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:57.020
So you need to be made aware
of these before you start

00:09:57.020 --> 00:10:02.000
your migration so you can plan
to mitigate any, I would say,

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:04.910
functionality that might
be missing in Postgres that

00:10:04.910 --> 00:10:08.420
you're currently using in Oracle
by leveraging other Google

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:10.910
Cloud technologies-- and we
see some examples of that--

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:14.400
or sometimes, even by writing
custom application code.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:16.580
So that's the first
thing that you

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:19.580
need to do when it comes
to database assessment

00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:21.530
and migration.

00:10:21.530 --> 00:10:23.990
You also need to be
able to determine,

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:27.320
when you do your
migrations to Google Cloud,

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:29.690
and specifically when you
choose cloud-native database

00:10:29.690 --> 00:10:34.070
technologies, such as
Postgres and/or MySQL,

00:10:34.070 --> 00:10:37.700
you also need to make a decision
which database engine is

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:38.600
better for you.

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:40.310
That's because a lot
of the functionality

00:10:40.310 --> 00:10:42.650
that you may have-- sorry,
I just escaped the slide.

00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:45.620
A lot of the functionality that
you may have in your source

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:48.940
Oracle database might only be
available in MySQL or Postgres.

00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:50.820
And we're going to talk
about that as well,

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:54.080
as well as some tools,
and products, and tips

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:56.760
that can help you make
the right decision.

00:10:56.760 --> 00:11:00.362
So assessment and source
discovery is very important.

00:11:00.362 --> 00:11:01.820
You'll need to
review your database

00:11:01.820 --> 00:11:04.710
configuration, your schema,
your database sizing.

00:11:04.710 --> 00:11:07.010
As Kavitha said, very
large, monolithic databases

00:11:07.010 --> 00:11:10.630
might need special attention
when it comes to migrations.

00:11:10.630 --> 00:11:12.470
You need to make sure
and see if you're

00:11:12.470 --> 00:11:14.330
using any proprietary features.

00:11:14.330 --> 00:11:16.460
You need to take a look
at your database workload,

00:11:16.460 --> 00:11:18.290
because some of your--
because as you know,

00:11:18.290 --> 00:11:20.390
applications which
were developed

00:11:20.390 --> 00:11:23.870
to run on an Oracle database
might use some Oracle-specific

00:11:23.870 --> 00:11:25.640
SQL syntax.

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:28.270
Or even when it comes to--

00:11:28.270 --> 00:11:32.400
if all of your applications
support other database engines,

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:35.630
such as Postgres, your workload
might behave differently.

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:37.320
So performance tuning
is also an aspect.

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:38.945
And we're going to
talk all about that.

00:11:38.945 --> 00:11:40.580
But assessment and
source discovery

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:42.740
is extremely,
extremely important.

00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:44.777
Also, as shown on
this slide, you

00:11:44.777 --> 00:11:46.610
have to understand what
applications connect

00:11:46.610 --> 00:11:48.027
to your database,
because when you

00:11:48.027 --> 00:11:50.780
move a database to the cloud,
you're saying, you know what?

00:11:50.780 --> 00:11:53.270
I'm going to move my Oracle
database to Cloud SQL Postgres,

00:11:53.270 --> 00:11:54.608
for example.

00:11:54.608 --> 00:11:56.900
You need to make sure you
understand which applications

00:11:56.900 --> 00:11:58.070
connect the database.

00:11:58.070 --> 00:12:00.800
Can these applications
be ported to Postgres?

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:03.050
Maybe they are
compatible with Postgres.

00:12:03.050 --> 00:12:05.520
If you have legacy
applications, it's

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:07.430
going to be a much more
challenging migration

00:12:07.430 --> 00:12:10.160
process than if your
applications are relatively

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:11.040
modern.

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:13.610
So the assessment
and source discovery

00:12:13.610 --> 00:12:16.220
is very, very, very important.

00:12:16.220 --> 00:12:19.350
And there are two ways
to actually do that.

00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:22.118
So the old-fashioned
way of figuring out

00:12:22.118 --> 00:12:23.660
what you have running
right now-- you

00:12:23.660 --> 00:12:25.850
might have a CMDB
database somewhere.

00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:29.210
But drilling down to
the actual databases,

00:12:29.210 --> 00:12:31.430
understanding what are the
features that you have,

00:12:31.430 --> 00:12:34.100
the configuration,
what high-availability

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:35.780
infrastructure you
have in place--

00:12:35.780 --> 00:12:37.488
you can either do it
by running scripts--

00:12:37.488 --> 00:12:38.480
you know, SQL scripts.

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:40.707
And you have a bunch
of code that you

00:12:40.707 --> 00:12:42.290
can run on the
database to detect what

00:12:42.290 --> 00:12:43.415
are the features are using.

00:12:43.415 --> 00:12:45.680
You can query the
Oracle Data dictionary

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:48.650
for understanding what
schema objects you use.

00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:51.920
You can query the
Oracle AWR repository

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:54.020
to understand what's
the workload running

00:12:54.020 --> 00:12:55.190
in the database.

00:12:55.190 --> 00:12:57.802
But we also want to
talk about the product

00:12:57.802 --> 00:12:58.760
that we have developed.

00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:00.968
As I said, we've been doing
these database migrations

00:13:00.968 --> 00:13:03.260
for a long period of
time, which kind of takes

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:05.720
all of our experience
and puts it

00:13:05.720 --> 00:13:08.053
in a product that can
automate the assessment

00:13:08.053 --> 00:13:08.970
and discovery for you.

00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:11.540
And that's migVisor, which
I'm going to show you a demo.

00:13:11.540 --> 00:13:14.180
migVisor for Google Cloud,
which we are officially

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:18.810
launching today, is
a custom-made product

00:13:18.810 --> 00:13:21.570
built for Google Cloud, which
can scan your existing Oracle

00:13:21.570 --> 00:13:24.570
environment and help you create
your migration plan, show you

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:27.420
what databases you have
running, their configuration,

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:29.670
the proprietary
features, show you

00:13:29.670 --> 00:13:32.880
the migration complexity
for both Postgres and MySQL

00:13:32.880 --> 00:13:33.520
running in GCP.

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:36.250
And we'll see a live demo,
which I think is very cool.

00:13:36.250 --> 00:13:37.950
But that's a-- we
think, at least,

00:13:37.950 --> 00:13:39.810
that's the reason
we created migVisor.

00:13:39.810 --> 00:13:41.940
It's a much faster
way and a better way

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:44.850
to start off your migration
journey, instead of having

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:47.740
to do everything manually.

00:13:47.740 --> 00:13:50.560
And as I mentioned previously,
it's also extremely important

00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:53.570
when you start your migration,
before you actually--

00:13:53.570 --> 00:13:56.050
and we'll talk about it in the
next steps in the migration

00:13:56.050 --> 00:13:56.660
process--

00:13:56.660 --> 00:13:58.840
but before you start
converting your schema

00:13:58.840 --> 00:14:01.600
and moving your data
and kind of decide--

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:03.520
actually implementing
the migration itself.

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:05.797
You have to choose
between-- if you're

00:14:05.797 --> 00:14:07.630
going to leverage
cloud-native technologies,

00:14:07.630 --> 00:14:09.590
if you're going to
leverage Google Cloud SQL,

00:14:09.590 --> 00:14:11.590
if you're going to leverage
open-source database

00:14:11.590 --> 00:14:14.110
engine, you have to choose
between Postgres and MySQL,

00:14:14.110 --> 00:14:17.650
which, again, depending on the
specific proprietary Oracle

00:14:17.650 --> 00:14:19.780
features you're using, the
types of schema objects

00:14:19.780 --> 00:14:24.740
that you'll have, Postgres or
MySQL might be a better fit.

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:26.350
And again, our
product, migVisor can

00:14:26.350 --> 00:14:30.910
help you make that decision,
as you'll see in a moment.

00:14:30.910 --> 00:14:32.410
But again, this is
an important step

00:14:32.410 --> 00:14:35.000
you have to take into account.

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:38.800
In addition to the structure
of your source database

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:41.170
and the compatibility of
certain schema objects,

00:14:41.170 --> 00:14:43.610
you also have to kind
of take into account

00:14:43.610 --> 00:14:45.630
if your IT staff is more
familiar with Postgres

00:14:45.630 --> 00:14:46.240
or MySQL.

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:48.717
These are the
non-technical requirements.

00:14:48.717 --> 00:14:50.050
But they're also very important.

00:14:50.050 --> 00:14:52.210
And also, your
applications-- so if you're

00:14:52.210 --> 00:14:55.000
moving your database and
its application to GCP,

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:57.670
you're going to leverage Cloud
SQL Postgres, for example.

00:14:57.670 --> 00:15:00.430
But the applications
that run on this database

00:15:00.430 --> 00:15:02.350
right now happen to
be, I don't know,

00:15:02.350 --> 00:15:04.730
support both Oracle and MySQL.

00:15:04.730 --> 00:15:06.790
So maybe MySQL will
be the better option.

00:15:06.790 --> 00:15:08.950
So also, you have to
take that into account

00:15:08.950 --> 00:15:12.240
as you start planning
your migration journey.

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:15.130
So there are-- on
the technical aspects

00:15:15.130 --> 00:15:16.840
of choosing between
Postgres and MySQL--

00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:19.060
and again, this talk is
mostly focused on Postgres.

00:15:19.060 --> 00:15:22.360
But it's still something
that's important to mention.

00:15:22.360 --> 00:15:25.090
So, as I said, there
are schema objects

00:15:25.090 --> 00:15:28.480
that you might be using
right now in Oracle

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:31.480
or features that you might be
using right now in your Oracle

00:15:31.480 --> 00:15:35.550
database that are supported
in either MySQL and Postgres.

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:38.560
You see some examples here, such
as sequences, which are only

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:41.138
supported in Postgres,
user-defined types, which

00:15:41.138 --> 00:15:42.430
are only supported in Postgres.

00:15:42.430 --> 00:15:44.450
But virtual columns,
on the other hand,

00:15:44.450 --> 00:15:46.300
have better support in MySQL.

00:15:46.300 --> 00:15:49.950
Partitions, for
example, Postgres 10

00:15:49.950 --> 00:15:51.730
is the first version
of Postgres that

00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:53.410
added declarative
partitioning support.

00:15:53.410 --> 00:15:55.840
Postgres 11 expands on that.

00:15:55.840 --> 00:15:58.120
But MySQL always had a
partitioning mechanism

00:15:58.120 --> 00:16:02.320
that more closely resembles what
you originally had in Oracle.

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:03.850
So there are differences.

00:16:03.850 --> 00:16:06.780
And it's very important to
kind of keep that in mind.

00:16:06.780 --> 00:16:08.330
And this is just a few of them.

00:16:08.330 --> 00:16:09.340
There are a bunch more.

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:11.465
And again, we're going to
show you in migVisor demo

00:16:11.465 --> 00:16:14.560
how it can help you-- how our
product can help you identify

00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:16.360
those features and
kind of tell you, hey,

00:16:16.360 --> 00:16:18.130
so this database
might be better suited

00:16:18.130 --> 00:16:21.160
to run on Cloud SQL Postgres,
while that database might

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:24.760
be more compatible
with Cloud SQL MySQL.

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:27.640
So again, just keep that in
mind as you start your migration

00:16:27.640 --> 00:16:29.400
journey.

00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:32.790
So migVisor for Google
Cloud, which, again, we

00:16:32.790 --> 00:16:36.670
are launching today, is meant
to automate your source target

00:16:36.670 --> 00:16:39.670
discovery and assessment phase.

00:16:39.670 --> 00:16:43.360
It detects your database
configuration, your schema

00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:45.520
configuration, the schema
object that you're using.

00:16:45.520 --> 00:16:48.010
It gives you an
overview of, hey, this

00:16:48.010 --> 00:16:49.720
is your existing Oracle fleet.

00:16:49.720 --> 00:16:52.292
These are the proprietary
features that you're using.

00:16:52.292 --> 00:16:54.250
These are the schema
objects that you're using,

00:16:54.250 --> 00:16:58.480
and then assigns a migration
compatibility score, migration

00:16:58.480 --> 00:17:02.770
complexity score, to both
Cloud SQL MySQL and Cloud SQL

00:17:02.770 --> 00:17:04.450
Postgres, as well
as, by the way,

00:17:04.450 --> 00:17:07.060
if you just plan to run
Postgres on a Compute Engine.

00:17:07.060 --> 00:17:08.560
So there are also
differences there,

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:10.170
which we'll talk
about in a moment.

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:12.670
So it's meant to automate this
process and kind of give you,

00:17:12.670 --> 00:17:15.910
in a very visual way, a lot
of insights, and metrics,

00:17:15.910 --> 00:17:18.400
and dashboards that are
relevant for a migration process

00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:21.550
before you even start migrating
a single database, which

00:17:21.550 --> 00:17:22.861
is, again, super important.

00:17:22.861 --> 00:17:24.319
You don't want to
start a migration

00:17:24.319 --> 00:17:27.579
and then, as you go through
it, kind of realize, oh, wow,

00:17:27.579 --> 00:17:30.070
we forgot that this application
connects the database once

00:17:30.070 --> 00:17:31.307
a quarter to run a report.

00:17:31.307 --> 00:17:32.890
And it's not compatible
with Postgres.

00:17:32.890 --> 00:17:34.870
So that puts a
stick in our wheels.

00:17:34.870 --> 00:17:37.660
Or oh, wow, we have
so many synonyms.

00:17:37.660 --> 00:17:39.460
We have like over
100,000 synonyms

00:17:39.460 --> 00:17:41.830
in our database, which--
we have syn databases that

00:17:41.830 --> 00:17:43.798
have such excessive
number of synonyms.

00:17:43.798 --> 00:17:45.340
And that's not
supported in Postgres.

00:17:45.340 --> 00:17:46.090
So what do we do?

00:17:46.090 --> 00:17:48.150
Should we create
views as a workaround?

00:17:48.150 --> 00:17:51.520
So all of that is kind
of presented in migVisor.

00:17:51.520 --> 00:17:53.540
Our product has two components.

00:17:53.540 --> 00:17:56.120
So it has the web console.

00:17:56.120 --> 00:17:57.180
It's a SaaS product.

00:17:57.180 --> 00:17:58.597
So you have the
web console, where

00:17:58.597 --> 00:18:00.850
you see all of our
migration insights, all

00:18:00.850 --> 00:18:03.030
of the details about
your environment,

00:18:03.030 --> 00:18:05.890
so we can explore and
see how your migration is

00:18:05.890 --> 00:18:07.330
going to look like.

00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:11.140
And also, we have a very
small standalone component

00:18:11.140 --> 00:18:14.230
known as the migVisor
Metadata Collector, which

00:18:14.230 --> 00:18:16.120
is a standalone Java utility.

00:18:16.120 --> 00:18:20.200
You need to download and run
it on a server in your existing

00:18:20.200 --> 00:18:20.710
environment.

00:18:20.710 --> 00:18:22.060
It can even be your laptop.

00:18:22.060 --> 00:18:25.000
You do not need to install
it on the database itself.

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:27.490
Our Metadata Collector,
what it basically does

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:30.310
is connects-- opens a JDBC
connection to your existing

00:18:30.310 --> 00:18:31.510
fleet of Oracle databases.

00:18:31.510 --> 00:18:34.190
It can connect to thousands
of databases in one go.

00:18:34.190 --> 00:18:38.220
So it supports analyzing
your databases in parallel,

00:18:38.220 --> 00:18:40.720
runs a bunch of queries, queries
the Oracle Data dictionary,

00:18:40.720 --> 00:18:44.020
queries the Oracle metadata
tables, the Oracle AWR

00:18:44.020 --> 00:18:46.750
repository, gathers all
of the information that's

00:18:46.750 --> 00:18:48.730
needed for the
migration analysis,

00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:51.730
and then creates a local
ZIP file, encrypted, which

00:18:51.730 --> 00:18:53.145
you upload to our console.

00:18:53.145 --> 00:18:53.770
And then, boom.

00:18:53.770 --> 00:18:55.990
You see all of the
migration insights.

00:18:55.990 --> 00:18:58.240
So that's kind of the
architecture for our product.

00:18:58.240 --> 00:19:01.030
And we created it so
it would be extremely

00:19:01.030 --> 00:19:02.082
easy and simple to use.

00:19:02.082 --> 00:19:03.790
Again, you don't need
to install anything

00:19:03.790 --> 00:19:05.170
on your source databases.

00:19:05.170 --> 00:19:06.910
You only need one
Metadata Collector,

00:19:06.910 --> 00:19:10.270
no matter how many Oracle
databases you want to analyze.

00:19:10.270 --> 00:19:14.950
And we see our users, when you
want to analyze your Oracle

00:19:14.950 --> 00:19:18.790
environment for Cloud SQL
migrations for GCP migrations,

00:19:18.790 --> 00:19:21.220
we see them being able
to-- from start to end,

00:19:21.220 --> 00:19:23.920
from actually being able to
access the migration insights,

00:19:23.920 --> 00:19:25.810
it's a matter of minutes, OK?

00:19:25.810 --> 00:19:27.550
And traditionally,
if you were to do

00:19:27.550 --> 00:19:30.668
this using scripts, and running
and creating spreadsheets

00:19:30.668 --> 00:19:32.710
and Word documents, kind
of mapping your existing

00:19:32.710 --> 00:19:35.510
environment and trying to figure
out which application connects

00:19:35.510 --> 00:19:37.510
to what database, and
what are the complexities,

00:19:37.510 --> 00:19:39.980
it would obviously take
much longer than that.

00:19:39.980 --> 00:19:42.460
Sometimes it's days,
minimum, sometimes even

00:19:42.460 --> 00:19:46.450
weeks for a large fleet
of Oracle databases.

00:19:46.450 --> 00:19:50.002
So before the demo, just
a couple of-- a few other

00:19:50.002 --> 00:19:51.460
slides I want to
talk to you about.

00:19:51.460 --> 00:19:54.450
So essentially, all of
our migration insights,

00:19:54.450 --> 00:19:56.670
all of our metrics, everything
that we kind of show

00:19:56.670 --> 00:20:00.030
and surface in migVisor,
it all boils down

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:01.960
to a migration score.

00:20:01.960 --> 00:20:04.720
So we basically give each
database in your environment

00:20:04.720 --> 00:20:09.660
a score from 0 to 100,
depending on how complex it is.

00:20:09.660 --> 00:20:11.910
So I just want to show a
couple of examples of--

00:20:11.910 --> 00:20:14.910
two examples of how the
low-complexity database

00:20:14.910 --> 00:20:17.420
for migration would look
like versus a high-complexity

00:20:17.420 --> 00:20:18.660
database.

00:20:18.660 --> 00:20:20.500
And you'll see more
in the live demo.

00:20:20.500 --> 00:20:23.600
So for example, we have an
easy migration database,

00:20:23.600 --> 00:20:25.210
an easy database to migrate.

00:20:25.210 --> 00:20:27.330
So this is an Oracle
database that has,

00:20:27.330 --> 00:20:29.820
of course, some
proprietary features.

00:20:29.820 --> 00:20:32.045
It has some Oracle-specific
schema objects.

00:20:32.045 --> 00:20:32.670
It makes sense.

00:20:32.670 --> 00:20:35.190
I mean, it would be rare
to see a production Oracle

00:20:35.190 --> 00:20:39.030
database that just has a bunch
of tables and indexes, right?

00:20:39.030 --> 00:20:42.100
So the features that we
detected in this database,

00:20:42.100 --> 00:20:46.950
such as a job scheduler,
inter-region Data Guard,

00:20:46.950 --> 00:20:49.890
certain tables and data types,
some functions or procedures,

00:20:49.890 --> 00:20:51.240
indexes--

00:20:51.240 --> 00:20:55.820
all of those aspects have
compatibility with, say,

00:20:55.820 --> 00:20:57.760
Postgres as a target platform.

00:20:57.760 --> 00:20:59.520
So the migration
score would be low.

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:00.820
And it would even--

00:21:00.820 --> 00:21:02.820
when we kind of show you
our migration insights,

00:21:02.820 --> 00:21:06.060
we'll even give you tips, what
we call migration tips, on,

00:21:06.060 --> 00:21:09.720
hey, you're using Oracle Job
Scheduler to maybe run jobs

00:21:09.720 --> 00:21:12.030
in your database on
a reoccurring basis.

00:21:12.030 --> 00:21:14.343
There are alternatives
you can use.

00:21:14.343 --> 00:21:15.510
So we're going to show you--

00:21:15.510 --> 00:21:17.140
maybe use Apache
Airflow, for example.

00:21:17.140 --> 00:21:19.860
So we're going to show you
that in our product as well.

00:21:19.860 --> 00:21:20.610
So it's very cool.

00:21:20.610 --> 00:21:22.027
And again, this
is just an example

00:21:22.027 --> 00:21:23.235
of a low-complexity database.

00:21:23.235 --> 00:21:25.860
We also look, by the way, at the
stored procedure code and kind

00:21:25.860 --> 00:21:28.380
of trying to figure out if you
have any usage of propriety

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:32.040
Oracle DBMS packages,
UTL packages, certain SQL

00:21:32.040 --> 00:21:35.400
syntax that might be only
compatible with Oracle,

00:21:35.400 --> 00:21:37.560
such as a connect-by
expression, which might not

00:21:37.560 --> 00:21:40.860
be supported fully.

00:21:40.860 --> 00:21:42.840
So again, a very easy
database to migrate.

00:21:42.840 --> 00:21:44.490
On the other hand,
there will also

00:21:44.490 --> 00:21:48.195
be databases which
would be more difficult.

00:21:48.195 --> 00:21:50.610
In a big Oracle
environment, you'll

00:21:50.610 --> 00:21:53.360
see a mix of low-complexity
and high-complexity databases.

00:21:53.360 --> 00:21:54.400
That is expected.

00:21:54.400 --> 00:21:56.460
But you want to be made
aware of that beforehand.

00:21:56.460 --> 00:21:58.260
And maybe that's how
you can prioritize

00:21:58.260 --> 00:21:59.760
which databases
you're going to move

00:21:59.760 --> 00:22:02.040
to cloud-native
open-source technologies

00:22:02.040 --> 00:22:04.350
and which databases
you might want to do

00:22:04.350 --> 00:22:05.800
as a lift-and-shift project.

00:22:05.800 --> 00:22:07.750
So here is a more
complex database.

00:22:07.750 --> 00:22:10.887
So it's a React database, which
would require rearchitecting

00:22:10.887 --> 00:22:12.720
your database for
deployment, because you're

00:22:12.720 --> 00:22:14.670
using an active ActiveCluster.

00:22:14.670 --> 00:22:16.870
It has a lot of database scenes.

00:22:16.870 --> 00:22:20.220
So you can have multiple stored
procedures or SQL queries

00:22:20.220 --> 00:22:23.340
where you're manually
influencing the behavior

00:22:23.340 --> 00:22:24.720
of the Oracle optimizer.

00:22:24.720 --> 00:22:27.397
So that would probably indicate
that performance tuning

00:22:27.397 --> 00:22:29.730
when you move to Postgres,
with its query planner, which

00:22:29.730 --> 00:22:32.190
would probably run
those SQLs differently,

00:22:32.190 --> 00:22:33.430
would require a big effort.

00:22:33.430 --> 00:22:35.472
So that's another thing
you need to keep in mind.

00:22:35.472 --> 00:22:37.800
It has synonyms, which are
not supported progress.

00:22:37.800 --> 00:22:40.260
But we will tell you, hey,
maybe move this to views.

00:22:40.260 --> 00:22:41.460
Migrate them to views.

00:22:41.460 --> 00:22:42.990
It has user directories.

00:22:42.990 --> 00:22:44.550
So you're probably
accessing files

00:22:44.550 --> 00:22:46.910
from the operating system
from within the database.

00:22:46.910 --> 00:22:48.660
So that would require,
again, maybe moving

00:22:48.660 --> 00:22:50.340
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:22:50.340 --> 00:22:51.600
Database links, same.

00:22:51.600 --> 00:22:52.790
So what are you going to do?

00:22:52.790 --> 00:22:55.373
You currently have this Oracle
database accessing other Oracle

00:22:55.373 --> 00:22:56.075
databases.

00:22:56.075 --> 00:22:57.450
So you need to
keep that in mind.

00:22:57.450 --> 00:23:00.000
Maybe you cannot move
this database in vacuum.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:03.110
And you need to maybe move a
bunch of databases together.

00:23:03.110 --> 00:23:07.710
And again, usage of DBMS and
UTL proprietory Oracle APIs

00:23:07.710 --> 00:23:11.160
inside your stored procedures,
multiple partitioned tables--

00:23:11.160 --> 00:23:13.260
some of them might
be hash partitioning,

00:23:13.260 --> 00:23:16.260
which is not supported-- only
supported in Postgres 11,

00:23:16.260 --> 00:23:17.720
if I remember correctly.

00:23:17.720 --> 00:23:20.980
Fast refresh materialized views,
complex queries-- so again,

00:23:20.980 --> 00:23:24.390
just an example of how would
a high-complexity database

00:23:24.390 --> 00:23:25.910
for migration would look like.

00:23:25.910 --> 00:23:28.410
And the benefit is that
you'll be able to determine,

00:23:28.410 --> 00:23:30.575
if you have multiple
Oracle databases, which

00:23:30.575 --> 00:23:31.950
are the ones that
are going to be

00:23:31.950 --> 00:23:34.450
the quick wins, the low-hanging
fruits, the ones you can get

00:23:34.450 --> 00:23:39.060
maybe migrating to Cloud SQL,
Postgres, and MySQL relatively

00:23:39.060 --> 00:23:40.810
easily.

00:23:40.810 --> 00:23:43.470
So let's maybe do a quick
demo of our product,

00:23:43.470 --> 00:23:47.397
just to show you
migVisor in live action.

00:23:47.397 --> 00:23:49.230
So if you can switch
to my computer, please,

00:23:49.230 --> 00:23:50.950
that would be fantastic.

00:23:50.950 --> 00:23:52.620
There we go.

00:23:52.620 --> 00:23:54.600
So this is our login screen.

00:23:54.600 --> 00:23:56.640
Again, it's a SaaS product.

00:23:56.640 --> 00:23:58.350
When you log in,
the first thing we

00:23:58.350 --> 00:24:00.350
show you is a
high-level overview

00:24:00.350 --> 00:24:03.670
of your current environment,
such as how many databases

00:24:03.670 --> 00:24:06.810
have you actually
analyzed using migVisor.

00:24:06.810 --> 00:24:09.660
This is just a very
small demo environment.

00:24:09.660 --> 00:24:12.870
The server sizings,
number of CPU cores--

00:24:12.870 --> 00:24:16.437
we also show you the comparative
migration complexity.

00:24:16.437 --> 00:24:18.020
Here, you can see
for running Postgres

00:24:18.020 --> 00:24:19.910
and Compute, versus
Cloud SQL Postgres,

00:24:19.910 --> 00:24:22.380
versus Cloud SQL MySQL.

00:24:22.380 --> 00:24:23.940
We also show you,
again, depending

00:24:23.940 --> 00:24:26.880
on the target that you choose,
a very high-level overview

00:24:26.880 --> 00:24:30.160
of the specific
complexity per database.

00:24:30.160 --> 00:24:32.490
But we do surface
a lot more details.

00:24:32.490 --> 00:24:34.700
This is just like a
high-level overview

00:24:34.700 --> 00:24:36.200
of your existing
environments so you

00:24:36.200 --> 00:24:40.350
will know what you're dealing
with before you dig deeper.

00:24:40.350 --> 00:24:41.850
And then I think
the coolest feature

00:24:41.850 --> 00:24:43.980
is that you can actually
simulate your migration

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:45.340
before you actually perform it.

00:24:45.340 --> 00:24:47.700
So say we're interested
in moving Oracle

00:24:47.700 --> 00:24:49.860
to Cloud SQL Postgres.

00:24:49.860 --> 00:24:51.580
That's what we're interested in.

00:24:51.580 --> 00:24:54.180
So we choose the source
and target combination.

00:24:54.180 --> 00:24:55.620
We click on Analyze.

00:24:55.620 --> 00:24:58.620
Then you can choose some of
all of our database server.

00:24:58.620 --> 00:24:59.760
Click on Analyze.

00:24:59.760 --> 00:25:02.970
And the first screen we see
is a very high-level screen

00:25:02.970 --> 00:25:05.070
that even if you're not
a database administrator,

00:25:05.070 --> 00:25:09.510
and you just have no database
background, what we do

00:25:09.510 --> 00:25:13.050
is rank your databases based
on migration complexity,

00:25:13.050 --> 00:25:14.230
from lowest to highest.

00:25:14.230 --> 00:25:17.397
So without even digging deeper,
which we'll see in a second,

00:25:17.397 --> 00:25:19.980
I know that they have databases
where the migration complexity

00:25:19.980 --> 00:25:21.300
is going to be very low.

00:25:21.300 --> 00:25:22.700
So maybe here is my POC.

00:25:22.700 --> 00:25:25.763
Here is my prototype database
for heterogeneous migration,

00:25:25.763 --> 00:25:27.180
versus other
databases, where it's

00:25:27.180 --> 00:25:28.750
going to be more complicated.

00:25:28.750 --> 00:25:31.030
So it doesn't mean that
we cannot migrate them.

00:25:31.030 --> 00:25:32.790
It just means it's
probably going to be

00:25:32.790 --> 00:25:34.210
a more challenging process.

00:25:34.210 --> 00:25:38.190
It's often worthwhile, but
just surfacing those details

00:25:38.190 --> 00:25:39.450
beforehand.

00:25:39.450 --> 00:25:41.920
Clicking on each one
of those databases,

00:25:41.920 --> 00:25:45.000
that's where you start to
see the verbose information.

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:47.460
So each one of those bars
is, of course, clickable.

00:25:47.460 --> 00:25:49.290
And you can also
kind of show all

00:25:49.290 --> 00:25:51.570
of the databases in one screen.

00:25:51.570 --> 00:25:53.280
And then, we actually--
this is where

00:25:53.280 --> 00:25:56.010
we present all of the
individual features

00:25:56.010 --> 00:25:58.020
that we have detected in
each one of the source

00:25:58.020 --> 00:26:02.250
databases that can make
your migration easy or more

00:26:02.250 --> 00:26:03.340
difficult.

00:26:03.340 --> 00:26:05.940
We also show you some
server-related information

00:26:05.940 --> 00:26:07.780
for sizing.

00:26:07.780 --> 00:26:09.780
Here are all the features
that we have detected.

00:26:09.780 --> 00:26:11.970
So this is, for example,
a record database.

00:26:11.970 --> 00:26:14.198
It has 103 database scenes.

00:26:14.198 --> 00:26:15.990
And it'll actually show
you the schema name

00:26:15.990 --> 00:26:19.050
and the actual
procedure name where

00:26:19.050 --> 00:26:20.730
we found the database scenes.

00:26:20.730 --> 00:26:22.620
It has over 1,000
synonyms, and so on.

00:26:22.620 --> 00:26:25.110
As you see, our list of the
features that we detect,

00:26:25.110 --> 00:26:27.600
which is over 100 different
features in Oracle,

00:26:27.600 --> 00:26:28.980
is quite extensive.

00:26:28.980 --> 00:26:33.390
And we also flag those features
based on the migration impact--

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:35.970
high, medium, and low.

00:26:35.970 --> 00:26:38.430
So it also takes into
account the number

00:26:38.430 --> 00:26:39.730
of usages for a feature.

00:26:39.730 --> 00:26:42.870
So it's not just, hey, we
found usage of Oracle's DBMS

00:26:42.870 --> 00:26:44.040
random package.

00:26:44.040 --> 00:26:46.060
It's because we found
only one usage of it.

00:26:46.060 --> 00:26:50.040
So even if you have to manually
code an equivalent for DBMS

00:26:50.040 --> 00:26:51.930
random or use the
Postgres equivalent,

00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:53.430
it's not going to
be that difficult.

00:26:53.430 --> 00:26:56.340
But if this was like, I don't
know, 1,000 occurrences,

00:26:56.340 --> 00:26:58.320
then it might be a little
bit more difficult.

00:26:58.320 --> 00:27:01.650
And we also provide
those migration tooltips.

00:27:01.650 --> 00:27:04.860
So when you click on this
little eye icon next to each one

00:27:04.860 --> 00:27:08.010
of the features that we detect,
we show you a small, short,

00:27:08.010 --> 00:27:12.190
and to-the-point tooltip
of saying, why is this--

00:27:12.190 --> 00:27:13.920
why was this feature flagged?

00:27:13.920 --> 00:27:15.210
Is it going to be a problem?

00:27:15.210 --> 00:27:16.290
Yes or no?

00:27:16.290 --> 00:27:20.010
How can you walk around
anything that's not supported?

00:27:20.010 --> 00:27:22.920
And you also provide links
to the actual Cloud SQL

00:27:22.920 --> 00:27:23.785
documentation.

00:27:23.785 --> 00:27:26.160
So, for example, for synonyms,
you'll see, clicking on it

00:27:26.160 --> 00:27:27.210
will just say, hey.

00:27:27.210 --> 00:27:28.647
So synonyms were detected.

00:27:28.647 --> 00:27:29.980
However, they are not supported.

00:27:29.980 --> 00:27:34.050
You can use views or maybe the
set search path in your queries

00:27:34.050 --> 00:27:35.100
as a workaround.

00:27:35.100 --> 00:27:37.080
And, again, we try to
provide this information

00:27:37.080 --> 00:27:39.960
for each one of the features
that we detect, provide

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:41.727
multiple alternatives
if possible,

00:27:41.727 --> 00:27:43.560
just so you'll know
that before you actually

00:27:43.560 --> 00:27:45.030
start your migration.

00:27:45.030 --> 00:27:47.933
We also provide the ability
to do schema-based analysis.

00:27:47.933 --> 00:27:49.350
So you can exclude
certain schemas

00:27:49.350 --> 00:27:51.060
from your migration analyses.

00:27:51.060 --> 00:27:53.310
Let's say you want to peel
off individual schemas

00:27:53.310 --> 00:27:55.890
and move them to GCP.

00:27:55.890 --> 00:27:58.290
We also kind of help you
perform target sizing.

00:27:58.290 --> 00:28:02.190
So we show you the memory,
IO, and CPU requirement

00:28:02.190 --> 00:28:04.470
for each schema, which can
be, again, very useful,

00:28:04.470 --> 00:28:06.053
because we see a use
case where people

00:28:06.053 --> 00:28:07.680
take these monolithic
Oracle databases

00:28:07.680 --> 00:28:11.850
and break them up, split them up
to smaller Cloud SQL instances.

00:28:11.850 --> 00:28:13.950
We also show you a list
of all of the applications

00:28:13.950 --> 00:28:14.850
that connect to the database.

00:28:14.850 --> 00:28:16.600
That's so you won't
forget any application

00:28:16.600 --> 00:28:17.850
when you do the migration.

00:28:17.850 --> 00:28:20.010
If there is this reporting
tool that connects once

00:28:20.010 --> 00:28:22.410
in a quarter, but generates
a report for a CEO,

00:28:22.410 --> 00:28:23.760
it's super critical.

00:28:23.760 --> 00:28:27.420
But that's a legacy application
written in Oracle Hyperion

00:28:27.420 --> 00:28:30.807
BI, something that's not
compatible with more modern

00:28:30.807 --> 00:28:31.890
cloud-native technologies.

00:28:31.890 --> 00:28:34.100
You want to be
made aware of that.

00:28:34.100 --> 00:28:35.730
We also show you--
we do a workload

00:28:35.730 --> 00:28:37.710
analysis on the
database, showing you

00:28:37.710 --> 00:28:40.470
on a per-schema level
and per-instance level,

00:28:40.470 --> 00:28:44.100
is this an OLTP database,
an analytics database,

00:28:44.100 --> 00:28:47.010
the ratio between OLTP and
analytics queries per schema.

00:28:47.010 --> 00:28:51.120
Maybe BigQuery would be a better
alternative for certain schemas

00:28:51.120 --> 00:28:54.157
where the workload is
predominantly analytics.

00:28:54.157 --> 00:28:55.490
So we show you this information.

00:28:55.490 --> 00:28:56.490
We kind of surface that.

00:28:56.490 --> 00:28:59.460
We also show you database
links, schema dependencies.

00:28:59.460 --> 00:29:01.100
So again, all of
this information

00:29:01.100 --> 00:29:02.780
is meant for you
to understand what

00:29:02.780 --> 00:29:05.540
you're dealing with before you
actually start your migration

00:29:05.540 --> 00:29:06.060
process.

00:29:06.060 --> 00:29:09.095
We also flag OCI applications
that connect to the database

00:29:09.095 --> 00:29:10.970
so that you'll know if
your application mixes

00:29:10.970 --> 00:29:14.630
modern or legacy.

00:29:14.630 --> 00:29:16.730
And again, we
encourage you to use--

00:29:16.730 --> 00:29:18.710
so migVisor GCP is
going to be made

00:29:18.710 --> 00:29:20.540
available in the
marketplace soon.

00:29:20.540 --> 00:29:21.915
Right now, if you
want to use it,

00:29:21.915 --> 00:29:23.870
you can reach out to Kavitha.

00:29:23.870 --> 00:29:25.910
And we do encourage
you to use migVisor

00:29:25.910 --> 00:29:29.300
for Google Cloud as the first
step in your migration process.

00:29:29.300 --> 00:29:31.970
We think it's very valuable
and can save you a lot of time.

00:29:31.970 --> 00:29:36.140
And we tried to just take
our experience and kind of--

00:29:36.140 --> 00:29:37.550
from database
migrations and kind

00:29:37.550 --> 00:29:40.110
of turn that into a product.

00:29:40.110 --> 00:29:41.640
So that's migVisor.

00:29:41.640 --> 00:29:43.940
If you can please maybe
switch back to the deck.

00:29:47.540 --> 00:29:48.435
There we go.

00:29:48.435 --> 00:29:50.060
So that's database
assessments for you.

00:29:50.060 --> 00:29:51.350
Again, it's the first step.

00:29:51.350 --> 00:29:53.698
And we encourage you
to put effort into it

00:29:53.698 --> 00:29:55.490
so that you will
basically know what you're

00:29:55.490 --> 00:29:57.657
dealing with before you
actually start the migration

00:29:57.657 --> 00:29:58.770
process itself.

00:29:58.770 --> 00:30:01.050
The second step is
success criteria.

00:30:01.050 --> 00:30:03.600
So it might seem like trivial.

00:30:03.600 --> 00:30:05.938
But it's actually
very, very important.

00:30:05.938 --> 00:30:06.980
There are multiple steps.

00:30:06.980 --> 00:30:08.750
When you actually start
migrating your database,

00:30:08.750 --> 00:30:10.490
converting your database,
there are multiple steps

00:30:10.490 --> 00:30:11.900
you have to take into account.

00:30:11.900 --> 00:30:14.108
You have to make sure that
all of your schema objects

00:30:14.108 --> 00:30:15.330
were converted successfully.

00:30:15.330 --> 00:30:16.820
You need to plan for
functionality testing.

00:30:16.820 --> 00:30:17.780
So you moved your database.

00:30:17.780 --> 00:30:18.863
You converted your schema.

00:30:18.863 --> 00:30:21.020
We'll talk about schema
conversion in a moment.

00:30:21.020 --> 00:30:24.530
You want to run a representative
test of your applications,

00:30:24.530 --> 00:30:26.580
of your current
application workload,

00:30:26.580 --> 00:30:29.810
on the target database to see
that nothing breaks, right?

00:30:29.810 --> 00:30:32.280
You want to make sure you
plan for data validation.

00:30:32.280 --> 00:30:34.917
So, as you move your data
from your on-prem environment

00:30:34.917 --> 00:30:37.250
to Cloud SQL, you want to
make sure that all of the data

00:30:37.250 --> 00:30:38.390
has been replicated
successfully.

00:30:38.390 --> 00:30:39.820
And we'll talk about
those steps in a moment.

00:30:39.820 --> 00:30:41.690
This is just
understanding what you

00:30:41.690 --> 00:30:43.640
need to do for you
to be successful

00:30:43.640 --> 00:30:44.840
and planning for that.

00:30:44.840 --> 00:30:46.460
You need to do
performance testing.

00:30:46.460 --> 00:30:49.250
Even if your applications are
fully compatible with Postgres,

00:30:49.250 --> 00:30:52.160
just switching the JDBC driver,
and everything will run fine,

00:30:52.160 --> 00:30:56.270
you're only using NC SQL
syntax, so no propriety Oracle

00:30:56.270 --> 00:30:57.950
joint syntax, or
all-up functions,

00:30:57.950 --> 00:30:59.370
or none of that stuff.

00:30:59.370 --> 00:31:01.490
Even with all of
that, the queries

00:31:01.490 --> 00:31:03.420
will behave differently--
different databases,

00:31:03.420 --> 00:31:04.760
different query optimizer.

00:31:04.760 --> 00:31:06.242
The execution plans will change.

00:31:06.242 --> 00:31:08.450
And queries that might have
behaved in a certain way,

00:31:08.450 --> 00:31:09.908
maybe using indexes,
might suddenly

00:31:09.908 --> 00:31:12.015
switch to full
table scans, right?

00:31:12.015 --> 00:31:14.390
So you have to make sure you
plan for performance testing

00:31:14.390 --> 00:31:14.890
as well.

00:31:14.890 --> 00:31:17.040
It's super important.

00:31:17.040 --> 00:31:19.080
You have to also plan
for integration testing.

00:31:19.080 --> 00:31:21.792
So as I mentioned, a
database is not in vacuum.

00:31:21.792 --> 00:31:23.750
It doesn't exist in
vacuum, because in addition

00:31:23.750 --> 00:31:25.167
to the application,
you might have

00:31:25.167 --> 00:31:27.697
dependencies on other
databases, or other technologies

00:31:27.697 --> 00:31:29.780
in your organization using
Oracle Database things,

00:31:29.780 --> 00:31:33.950
or application-tier
integration APIs

00:31:33.950 --> 00:31:37.950
that need to query multiple
databases at the same time.

00:31:37.950 --> 00:31:40.112
So you have to plan to
make those tests as well.

00:31:40.112 --> 00:31:41.570
And, again, the
same tests that you

00:31:41.570 --> 00:31:42.750
have to do on the
database level,

00:31:42.750 --> 00:31:44.660
you also have to do that
for the application, so unit

00:31:44.660 --> 00:31:46.777
testing, performance
testing, integration testing.

00:31:46.777 --> 00:31:48.110
Again, and plan for all of that.

00:31:48.110 --> 00:31:53.780
That's your success criteria
for migration right here.

00:31:53.780 --> 00:31:55.730
The next step would
be to actually

00:31:55.730 --> 00:31:59.600
start getting your fingers
dirty and actually starting

00:31:59.600 --> 00:32:02.690
doing the migration itself.

00:32:02.690 --> 00:32:06.050
That normally starts from
the environment setups.

00:32:06.050 --> 00:32:09.470
You have to kind of create
the target environment in GCP.

00:32:09.470 --> 00:32:13.250
That means that you have to
first convert your schema.

00:32:13.250 --> 00:32:18.895
And that's a hot potato, because
we can detect, using migVisor,

00:32:18.895 --> 00:32:20.270
for example, a
lot of proprietary

00:32:20.270 --> 00:32:21.500
features you're using.

00:32:21.500 --> 00:32:22.790
We can surface those details.

00:32:22.790 --> 00:32:24.897
We can give you tips
on what you have to do.

00:32:24.897 --> 00:32:27.230
But you'll still need to
actually perform the migration.

00:32:27.230 --> 00:32:29.300
So you have to leverage
different APIs,

00:32:29.300 --> 00:32:31.130
convert your stored
procedures, convert

00:32:31.130 --> 00:32:35.120
your materialized views, for
example, your partition tables,

00:32:35.120 --> 00:32:36.290
all of that stuff.

00:32:36.290 --> 00:32:38.810
Now, there are tools,
such as ora2pg,

00:32:38.810 --> 00:32:40.430
which is an
open-source tool, that

00:32:40.430 --> 00:32:44.870
can help you automate a lot of
the schema conversion process.

00:32:44.870 --> 00:32:47.000
Depending on the complexity
of the source database,

00:32:47.000 --> 00:32:48.583
the success rate,
from our experience,

00:32:48.583 --> 00:32:51.920
go anywhere from like 30%,
40% automatic conversion

00:32:51.920 --> 00:32:54.470
to sometimes even 60% and 70%.

00:32:54.470 --> 00:32:56.400
But it's never 100%.

00:32:56.400 --> 00:32:57.980
So for the schema
conversion piece,

00:32:57.980 --> 00:33:01.100
leverage automatic
tools such as ora2pg.

00:33:01.100 --> 00:33:03.630
But you'll also need to put
some elbow grease into it.

00:33:03.630 --> 00:33:06.710
You have to put some
manual, honest DBA

00:33:06.710 --> 00:33:09.770
work to kind of convert some
of the more trickier schema

00:33:09.770 --> 00:33:12.050
objects, some of the more
trickier stored procedures.

00:33:12.050 --> 00:33:13.820
So that's schema conversion.

00:33:13.820 --> 00:33:15.320
It's part of your
environment setup.

00:33:15.320 --> 00:33:17.778
Before you move the data, before
you test your application,

00:33:17.778 --> 00:33:20.690
you need to make sure you have
a fully converted source schema.

00:33:20.690 --> 00:33:22.280
The second aspect
is, well, you'll

00:33:22.280 --> 00:33:24.350
have to deploy your
Cloud SQL target.

00:33:24.350 --> 00:33:26.990
So you have to understand
what's the configure-- what's

00:33:26.990 --> 00:33:29.000
the sizing that you
want your Cloud SQL

00:33:29.000 --> 00:33:30.770
target to be comprised of?

00:33:30.770 --> 00:33:32.270
So how many CPUs cores?

00:33:32.270 --> 00:33:33.740
How much memory?

00:33:33.740 --> 00:33:35.597
What's the IO requirements?

00:33:35.597 --> 00:33:38.180
If you're a breaking one Oracle
database to multiple Cloud SQL

00:33:38.180 --> 00:33:40.850
instances, you also have
to keep that in mind.

00:33:40.850 --> 00:33:42.800
So you have to
size intelligently.

00:33:42.800 --> 00:33:44.990
That's what we always say.

00:33:44.990 --> 00:33:46.310
And you can reference--

00:33:46.310 --> 00:33:49.607
I would say, as a tip,
reference the workload

00:33:49.607 --> 00:33:52.190
you have on your Oracle database
and not the physical hardware

00:33:52.190 --> 00:33:54.050
configuration as your baseline.

00:33:54.050 --> 00:33:56.750
That's because cloud
economics for scaling

00:33:56.750 --> 00:33:59.060
are different from
on-premise economics.

00:33:59.060 --> 00:34:00.860
And we see, a lot
of times, we have

00:34:00.860 --> 00:34:03.980
these big, monolithic Oracle
databases running on--

00:34:03.980 --> 00:34:05.270
which have been over-deployed.

00:34:05.270 --> 00:34:11.679
So an organization bought a very
high-end, super capable server

00:34:11.679 --> 00:34:14.362
with hundreds of CPU
cores, terabytes of memory,

00:34:14.362 --> 00:34:16.070
just in case, because
they know that they

00:34:16.070 --> 00:34:17.880
might need to scale up.

00:34:17.880 --> 00:34:20.630
They know that they might need
room to grow in the future.

00:34:20.630 --> 00:34:22.370
So they have over-provisioned.

00:34:22.370 --> 00:34:24.949
Not all of the databases
are like that, but many are.

00:34:24.949 --> 00:34:28.949
And because it's very easy
to scale up when you move

00:34:28.949 --> 00:34:31.835
to cloud-native technologies,
we would encourage you,

00:34:31.835 --> 00:34:34.210
based on our experience, to
look at your actual workload,

00:34:34.210 --> 00:34:36.502
such as the number of active
sessions in your database,

00:34:36.502 --> 00:34:38.810
instead of physical CPU cores--

00:34:38.810 --> 00:34:41.480
the actual physical reads
from your storage tier,

00:34:41.480 --> 00:34:44.690
instead of the IOPS and
throughput capacity of your,

00:34:44.690 --> 00:34:48.284
I don't know, like
NetApp or storage machine

00:34:48.284 --> 00:34:49.159
in your organization.

00:34:49.159 --> 00:34:51.985
So base your sizing on workload.

00:34:51.985 --> 00:34:53.360
And again, we try
to kind of show

00:34:53.360 --> 00:34:56.250
this information-- some of it,
at least-- in migVisor as well.

00:34:56.250 --> 00:35:00.170
And then you also deploy your
actual Google Cloud targets,

00:35:00.170 --> 00:35:03.980
which includes, say, Cloud SQL
for Postgres for the database,

00:35:03.980 --> 00:35:07.575
but also compute engines,
VMs, for your applications,

00:35:07.575 --> 00:35:09.950
because you know, you're going
to test your applications.

00:35:09.950 --> 00:35:11.818
They need a virtual
machine to run.

00:35:11.818 --> 00:35:13.610
So you also have to
kind of do that as part

00:35:13.610 --> 00:35:16.730
of your environment setup.

00:35:16.730 --> 00:35:18.200
When it comes to
schema conversion,

00:35:18.200 --> 00:35:20.750
again, I've kind of talked
about it in a previous slide.

00:35:20.750 --> 00:35:24.140
But some tidbits
here when it comes

00:35:24.140 --> 00:35:27.590
to moving your different
database objects that you might

00:35:27.590 --> 00:35:30.110
have in your current schema
right now from Oracle

00:35:30.110 --> 00:35:31.800
to Postgres-- some
of the challenges--

00:35:31.800 --> 00:35:33.680
and you'll have
access to this slide,

00:35:33.680 --> 00:35:36.180
to this stack, so you can
kind of reference that later.

00:35:36.180 --> 00:35:39.320
But for example, object
identifiers-- so Oracle,

00:35:39.320 --> 00:35:41.480
basically, if you're
using quoted identifiers,

00:35:41.480 --> 00:35:42.903
it converts it to uppercase.

00:35:42.903 --> 00:35:44.570
And Postgres converts
them to lowercase.

00:35:44.570 --> 00:35:45.990
So this can break your SQLs.

00:35:45.990 --> 00:35:46.950
And less code, right?

00:35:46.950 --> 00:35:48.620
But we see quotes
and identifiers

00:35:48.620 --> 00:35:50.840
in a lot of the
queries generated

00:35:50.840 --> 00:35:51.860
by certain applications.

00:35:51.860 --> 00:35:55.310
So that's one thing that
can kind of cause pain when

00:35:55.310 --> 00:35:57.200
it comes to schema migrations.

00:35:57.200 --> 00:35:59.720
Database links, which we
talked about-- so Postgres has

00:35:59.720 --> 00:36:02.240
an extension, Foreign Data
Wrapper, that can give you

00:36:02.240 --> 00:36:04.730
equivalent database link
functionality in the database,

00:36:04.730 --> 00:36:05.510
right?

00:36:05.510 --> 00:36:07.950
That's not supported
in Cloud SQL right now.

00:36:07.950 --> 00:36:09.350
So you have to
keep that in mind.

00:36:09.350 --> 00:36:12.350
Maybe for certain
databases that are heavily

00:36:12.350 --> 00:36:13.880
relying on database
links, you might

00:36:13.880 --> 00:36:16.220
be better off with
Postgres on Compute Engine

00:36:16.220 --> 00:36:19.110
or moving some of that logic
to the application tier.

00:36:19.110 --> 00:36:22.580
Again, partitioned tables-- so
Postgres 9 has some limited--

00:36:22.580 --> 00:36:24.950
does not have declarative
partitioning support.

00:36:24.950 --> 00:36:27.050
It only provides
table inheritance,

00:36:27.050 --> 00:36:28.580
which is not equivalent.

00:36:28.580 --> 00:36:30.470
What Oracle does
is partitioning.

00:36:30.470 --> 00:36:34.040
Postgres then adds the support
for table partitioning,

00:36:34.040 --> 00:36:36.510
some of the Oracle partition
types, not all of them.

00:36:36.510 --> 00:36:38.950
Postgres 11 expands
that a bit further.

00:36:38.950 --> 00:36:40.790
But again, keep that in mind.

00:36:40.790 --> 00:36:42.410
Sequences-- so you
move your database.

00:36:42.410 --> 00:36:44.370
Sequence values will
have to be created.

00:36:44.370 --> 00:36:46.340
So you might have
sequences right now

00:36:46.340 --> 00:36:48.380
that have their values.

00:36:48.380 --> 00:36:50.420
So sequence A might be
at a value of 10,000.

00:36:50.420 --> 00:36:55.280
Sequence B might be currently
at a value of 256,091.

00:36:55.280 --> 00:36:57.260
So when you create the
sequences in the target,

00:36:57.260 --> 00:36:59.000
you have to make sure
that you actually--

00:36:59.000 --> 00:37:02.060
when it comes to the switchover,
you rethink your sequences.

00:37:02.060 --> 00:37:04.520
You advance them manually
to their current state

00:37:04.520 --> 00:37:06.020
in the source Oracle database.

00:37:06.020 --> 00:37:07.460
Again, these are just
some of the challenges

00:37:07.460 --> 00:37:08.918
you have to keep
in mind, even when

00:37:08.918 --> 00:37:11.210
you're doing a lot of
this schema conversion,

00:37:11.210 --> 00:37:13.040
using tools such as ora2pg.

00:37:13.040 --> 00:37:15.170
There is stuff that you
have to do manually.

00:37:15.170 --> 00:37:16.970
Triggers are also
different, right?

00:37:16.970 --> 00:37:21.470
So a trigger in Oracle is both
the event invoker as well as

00:37:21.470 --> 00:37:22.020
the logic.

00:37:22.020 --> 00:37:24.242
So you create a
trigger and a table.

00:37:24.242 --> 00:37:25.700
The trigger will
actually fire once

00:37:25.700 --> 00:37:27.828
when you insert a row,
for example, once per row.

00:37:27.828 --> 00:37:29.120
And it also contains the logic.

00:37:29.120 --> 00:37:30.380
In Postgres, it's different.

00:37:30.380 --> 00:37:32.547
You have the trigger itself,
which calls a function.

00:37:32.547 --> 00:37:35.580
So again, just kind
of keep that in mind.

00:37:35.580 --> 00:37:36.413
Some other aspects--

00:37:36.413 --> 00:37:38.038
I don't want to go
through all of that.

00:37:38.038 --> 00:37:39.710
That's a lot of
the nitty-gritty.

00:37:39.710 --> 00:37:42.300
But there are aspects when
it comes to schema conversion

00:37:42.300 --> 00:37:45.125
environment setup that you have
to kind of take into account.

00:37:45.125 --> 00:37:47.500
By they way, this is all kind
of surfaced in our product.

00:37:47.500 --> 00:37:50.960
But we thought it would be nice
to show some of those bigger

00:37:50.960 --> 00:37:53.960
challenges in the slide deck,
such as using views instead

00:37:53.960 --> 00:37:56.555
of virtual columns, local
temporary tables instead

00:37:56.555 --> 00:37:57.680
of global temporary tables.

00:37:57.680 --> 00:37:59.540
Temporary table
behavior is different.

00:37:59.540 --> 00:38:01.520
So keep that in mind as well.

00:38:01.520 --> 00:38:03.160
Reverse key indexes
are not supported.

00:38:03.160 --> 00:38:04.790
Bitmap indexes
are not supported.

00:38:04.790 --> 00:38:06.480
Synonyms, you have
to use views instead.

00:38:06.480 --> 00:38:09.500
So again, keep all of that in
mind as you do the migration.

00:38:09.500 --> 00:38:11.630
There are data type differences.

00:38:11.630 --> 00:38:12.420
Again, this is a--

00:38:12.420 --> 00:38:12.920
it's boring.

00:38:12.920 --> 00:38:13.660
It's nitty-gritty stuff.

00:38:13.660 --> 00:38:14.577
But keep that in mind.

00:38:14.577 --> 00:38:17.630
Not all data types
are supported as-is.

00:38:17.630 --> 00:38:20.060
Even Oracle LOBs-- if you
move them to the BYTEA data

00:38:20.060 --> 00:38:22.730
type in Postgres, it has
a 1-gigabyte limitation,

00:38:22.730 --> 00:38:24.030
require API changes.

00:38:24.030 --> 00:38:25.550
So again, keep that in mind.

00:38:25.550 --> 00:38:28.220
And it's very-- now you see why
it's important to of surface

00:38:28.220 --> 00:38:30.980
all of those details before
you actually start migrating,

00:38:30.980 --> 00:38:32.690
because you want
to choose-- maybe

00:38:32.690 --> 00:38:34.630
for the first database
you move to Cloud SQL,

00:38:34.630 --> 00:38:37.370
you want to choose a database
that's, I would say, easier

00:38:37.370 --> 00:38:39.912
to migrate and not necessarily
your most complicated database

00:38:39.912 --> 00:38:41.790
in the organization.

00:38:41.790 --> 00:38:44.990
Some other stuff, such
as certain changes

00:38:44.990 --> 00:38:48.750
in how you invoke functions,
certain changes in the SQL

00:38:48.750 --> 00:38:49.350
dialect.

00:38:49.350 --> 00:38:51.480
You'll have access to this deck.

00:38:51.480 --> 00:38:54.240
Just some tips, lesson
learned from migrations

00:38:54.240 --> 00:38:56.110
we have previously done.

00:38:56.110 --> 00:38:58.710
But again, it's just kind of
surfacing the challenges when

00:38:58.710 --> 00:39:03.240
it comes to schema migrations.

00:39:03.240 --> 00:39:04.990
A couple of things
about stored procedures

00:39:04.990 --> 00:39:06.490
I want to mentioned briefly--

00:39:06.490 --> 00:39:11.660
so Postgres 11-- sorry,
Postgres versions 9 and 10

00:39:11.660 --> 00:39:14.183
only provide functions
instead of packages

00:39:14.183 --> 00:39:15.100
and stored procedures.

00:39:15.100 --> 00:39:16.762
That's a challenge.

00:39:16.762 --> 00:39:18.220
Again, you have to
create functions

00:39:18.220 --> 00:39:19.690
instead of stored procedures.

00:39:19.690 --> 00:39:23.110
Postgres 11 finally adds a
CREATE PROCEDURE statement.

00:39:23.110 --> 00:39:24.410
So that's super cool.

00:39:24.410 --> 00:39:28.000
However, invoking functions
and procedures are different.

00:39:28.000 --> 00:39:30.850
So you need to change that
syntax in your application.

00:39:30.850 --> 00:39:32.620
And packages that
are not supported.

00:39:32.620 --> 00:39:34.540
So you need to use
schemas in Postgres

00:39:34.540 --> 00:39:38.170
to group certain procedures
or functions together.

00:39:38.170 --> 00:39:41.320
Certain Oracle PL/SQL
control elements

00:39:41.320 --> 00:39:42.620
are also not supported.

00:39:42.620 --> 00:39:44.920
So again, a lot of
stuff to take in.

00:39:44.920 --> 00:39:46.700
We don't have time
for all of that.

00:39:46.700 --> 00:39:50.080
But just keep that in mind when
you do your schema conversion.

00:39:50.080 --> 00:39:52.030
And again, reference this deck.

00:39:52.030 --> 00:39:56.285
And you can use our product
for some other information

00:39:56.285 --> 00:39:58.660
that could be-- can save you
a lot of pain in the future.

00:39:58.660 --> 00:40:01.150
So Postgres 11,
Postgres 10 and 11

00:40:01.150 --> 00:40:02.590
also brings a lot
of improvements

00:40:02.590 --> 00:40:05.260
when it comes to Oracle
compatibility versus Postgres

00:40:05.260 --> 00:40:07.825
9.6, such as enhancements
of parallel queries.

00:40:07.825 --> 00:40:09.200
Again, this is a
very nice slide.

00:40:09.200 --> 00:40:11.650
I kind of cherry picked some
of the features which I think

00:40:11.650 --> 00:40:14.350
are most applicable when
it comes to migrations.

00:40:14.350 --> 00:40:17.680
I kind of cherry picked those
and put them in one slide,

00:40:17.680 --> 00:40:19.240
so you can have
it as a reference,

00:40:19.240 --> 00:40:21.820
because Postgres 10 and 11, they
have a lot of functionality.

00:40:21.820 --> 00:40:24.430
Some of it is specifically
relevant for Oracle-to-Postgres

00:40:24.430 --> 00:40:25.390
migration.

00:40:25.390 --> 00:40:27.700
So enhancements in parallel
query, such as parallel

00:40:27.700 --> 00:40:29.458
merge join, such
as parallel index

00:40:29.458 --> 00:40:31.750
scan-- these are things that
the Oracle optimizer knows

00:40:31.750 --> 00:40:32.250
how to do.

00:40:32.250 --> 00:40:34.660
And now, Postgres knows
how to do them as well.

00:40:34.660 --> 00:40:38.620
We have full text search support
for JSON and JSONB data types.

00:40:38.620 --> 00:40:40.690
We have additional
performance enhancements,

00:40:40.690 --> 00:40:43.600
by the way, in Postgres 11,
such as paralyzed hash joints--

00:40:43.600 --> 00:40:44.920
super important.

00:40:44.920 --> 00:40:47.290
Covering indexes--
finally, right?

00:40:47.290 --> 00:40:49.940
So that's another
Postgres 11 feature.

00:40:49.940 --> 00:40:51.717
You can use CREATE
PROCEDURE, which

00:40:51.717 --> 00:40:53.800
is the new syntax, not
available previous to that.

00:40:53.800 --> 00:40:56.170
And you can call
using the CALL syntax

00:40:56.170 --> 00:40:58.990
to invoke stored procedures
in your database.

00:40:58.990 --> 00:41:01.390
But again, have this
slide as a reference.

00:41:01.390 --> 00:41:03.190
Keep in mind that
Postgres 10 and 11

00:41:03.190 --> 00:41:05.140
bring a lot of new
functionality that's

00:41:05.140 --> 00:41:08.230
more equivalent for what
you might have right

00:41:08.230 --> 00:41:10.130
now in your Oracle environment.

00:41:10.130 --> 00:41:12.550
OK, and when you finalize
all the schema conversion

00:41:12.550 --> 00:41:14.890
aspects, automatic
and manual, and you

00:41:14.890 --> 00:41:17.740
want to deploy your Cloud SQL
instance, it's super easy,

00:41:17.740 --> 00:41:20.922
like a few clicks in the
Google Cloud Console UI.

00:41:20.922 --> 00:41:22.630
You can provision a
Cloud SQL instance up

00:41:22.630 --> 00:41:26.338
to 64 cores and 416 gigabytes
of memory, which, by the way,

00:41:26.338 --> 00:41:27.380
that's the limit for you.

00:41:27.380 --> 00:41:30.880
So if you have an Oracle
database that has more,

00:41:30.880 --> 00:41:35.200
you need more than 64 cores
or 416 gigs of memory,

00:41:35.200 --> 00:41:37.270
you will probably
need to split up

00:41:37.270 --> 00:41:42.100
this database and its workload
to multiple smaller Cloud SQL

00:41:42.100 --> 00:41:44.800
instances in GCP.

00:41:44.800 --> 00:41:48.130
If you're migrating Oracle RAC,
it's going to be a challenge.

00:41:48.130 --> 00:41:51.190
There is no way to
do Active/Active

00:41:51.190 --> 00:41:54.790
clustering in GCP right now--

00:41:54.790 --> 00:41:56.460
in Postgres at all, by the way.

00:41:56.460 --> 00:41:58.390
It's not just specific to GCP.

00:41:58.390 --> 00:42:00.130
But there are
workarounds around that,

00:42:00.130 --> 00:42:03.070
such as moving
some of that logic

00:42:03.070 --> 00:42:08.110
into the data access layer, into
the application tier itself,

00:42:08.110 --> 00:42:09.730
so that you can
actually maybe have

00:42:09.730 --> 00:42:15.080
like a fleet of Postgres
Cloud SQL databases ready.

00:42:15.080 --> 00:42:19.390
And then you can do the hot-swap
via the application tier.

00:42:19.390 --> 00:42:21.710
You can also maybe send
application requests

00:42:21.710 --> 00:42:24.580
using a queue, such
as Google Pub/Sub,

00:42:24.580 --> 00:42:28.810
so that the data access layer
can, in case of failover,

00:42:28.810 --> 00:42:30.940
take those requests
and route them

00:42:30.940 --> 00:42:33.940
to the Cloud SQL instance
that will become available.

00:42:33.940 --> 00:42:38.110
So there are some workarounds
about that as well.

00:42:38.110 --> 00:42:40.660
Oracle Data Guard is going to
be a bit easier to migrate.

00:42:40.660 --> 00:42:43.030
You can use Postgres
replication, which is natively

00:42:43.030 --> 00:42:44.950
supported in Cloud SQL.

00:42:44.950 --> 00:42:47.560
There are some challenges,
such as cross-region DR,

00:42:47.560 --> 00:42:49.253
if you're doing that right now.

00:42:49.253 --> 00:42:50.420
But again, it's much easier.

00:42:50.420 --> 00:42:52.720
So RAC is going to be tricky,
require some application

00:42:52.720 --> 00:42:54.512
changes if you want to
meet the same levels

00:42:54.512 --> 00:42:55.570
of high availability.

00:42:55.570 --> 00:42:57.452
Data Guard is a bit easier.

00:42:57.452 --> 00:42:58.660
But again, keep that in mind.

00:42:58.660 --> 00:43:00.003
It still needs to--

00:43:00.003 --> 00:43:02.170
you need to know how to
leverage the right Cloud SQL

00:43:02.170 --> 00:43:05.020
technologies to do that.

00:43:05.020 --> 00:43:07.570
You'll need to
test for failover.

00:43:07.570 --> 00:43:11.590
So before you actually sign
off on the new environment,

00:43:11.590 --> 00:43:12.715
you have to test failover.

00:43:12.715 --> 00:43:14.090
So once you
provision everything,

00:43:14.090 --> 00:43:16.030
once you have your
replication set up,

00:43:16.030 --> 00:43:17.920
once you have your high
availability set up,

00:43:17.920 --> 00:43:20.410
you have to test failover
before you move your production

00:43:20.410 --> 00:43:20.910
workload.

00:43:20.910 --> 00:43:24.130
You have to see that it behaves
as you expect it to behave.

00:43:24.130 --> 00:43:25.400
You have to test your backups.

00:43:25.400 --> 00:43:29.128
You have to test how long it
takes to recover your database.

00:43:29.128 --> 00:43:30.670
And you have to, of
course, configure

00:43:30.670 --> 00:43:31.810
monitoring and alerting.

00:43:31.810 --> 00:43:33.610
This is all part of
the environment setup.

00:43:33.610 --> 00:43:37.130
And again it's super,
super, super important.

00:43:37.130 --> 00:43:42.885
Other step-- now, I'm not going
to go into the next few steps.

00:43:42.885 --> 00:43:44.200
A, I don't have a lot of time.

00:43:44.200 --> 00:43:48.280
But B, there are other talks at
Next which cover these aspects.

00:43:48.280 --> 00:43:50.890
There is a great talk from
Alooma about data replication

00:43:50.890 --> 00:43:51.760
specifically.

00:43:51.760 --> 00:43:53.410
I just want to
briefly mention that.

00:43:53.410 --> 00:43:54.840
So data migration.

00:43:54.840 --> 00:43:56.590
So after you've
provisioned your Cloud SQL

00:43:56.590 --> 00:43:58.390
target, converted
the schema, it's

00:43:58.390 --> 00:44:00.090
going to be a challenge, right?

00:44:00.090 --> 00:44:02.090
You still need to take
care of moving your data,

00:44:02.090 --> 00:44:04.465
copying your data from your
Oracle database to the cloud,

00:44:04.465 --> 00:44:05.730
to Postgres.

00:44:05.730 --> 00:44:09.290
There are a lot of tools that do
heterogeneous data replication.

00:44:09.290 --> 00:44:12.050
So you can either use a
bunch of, like, ETL tools

00:44:12.050 --> 00:44:13.070
that are available--

00:44:13.070 --> 00:44:14.130
Informatica, for example.

00:44:14.130 --> 00:44:18.030
You might be using Pentaho,
or Talend, or CloverETL.

00:44:18.030 --> 00:44:18.885
There are a lot of--

00:44:18.885 --> 00:44:20.510
some open-source,
some commercial tools

00:44:20.510 --> 00:44:22.790
that can help you
move your data.

00:44:22.790 --> 00:44:25.190
But you also need to
understand the concept

00:44:25.190 --> 00:44:28.590
of CDC data replication,
which is Change Data Capture.

00:44:28.590 --> 00:44:31.280
So if you're using an ETL
tool to move your data,

00:44:31.280 --> 00:44:32.870
that's a one-time copy.

00:44:32.870 --> 00:44:35.390
And that would require
downtime when moving your data

00:44:35.390 --> 00:44:37.130
from your existing
Oracle environment

00:44:37.130 --> 00:44:39.190
to Postgres, because
you move the data,

00:44:39.190 --> 00:44:40.190
but data keeps changing.

00:44:40.190 --> 00:44:42.110
If people keep working on
your source Oracle database,

00:44:42.110 --> 00:44:43.230
data keeps changing.

00:44:43.230 --> 00:44:46.190
So you will lose data
on the switch over.

00:44:46.190 --> 00:44:47.960
And that's why
tools that perform

00:44:47.960 --> 00:44:51.290
real-time CDC, Change Data
Capture-- kind of like they

00:44:51.290 --> 00:44:54.140
read the Oracle, read
your logs, and they stream

00:44:54.140 --> 00:44:56.420
the transactions to the
target in real time,

00:44:56.420 --> 00:44:58.835
are often used when you
want to minimize downtime.

00:44:58.835 --> 00:45:01.460
Heck, many of you might be using
products such as Oracle Golden

00:45:01.460 --> 00:45:03.830
Gate right now, which is
a heterogeneous database

00:45:03.830 --> 00:45:05.450
replication product from Oracle.

00:45:05.450 --> 00:45:08.490
You can leverage that to move
data to Postgres, for example.

00:45:08.490 --> 00:45:10.760
So there are a lot
of tools out there.

00:45:10.760 --> 00:45:13.760
And there is a great
Alooma talk about data

00:45:13.760 --> 00:45:17.420
replication at Next, which
I encourage you to attend.

00:45:17.420 --> 00:45:19.700
But then again, keep
in mind the differences

00:45:19.700 --> 00:45:24.472
between one-time loads
and real-time replication.

00:45:24.472 --> 00:45:26.430
So again, I talked about
it in previous slides.

00:45:26.430 --> 00:45:27.680
So I'm not going to reiterate.

00:45:27.680 --> 00:45:30.270
But you need to make sure
that you take into account

00:45:30.270 --> 00:45:32.520
both loading what you currently
have in your database,

00:45:32.520 --> 00:45:34.770
as well as kind of how you're
going to plan and stream

00:45:34.770 --> 00:45:36.740
changes to the target.

00:45:36.740 --> 00:45:38.400
You'll need to test
your applications.

00:45:38.400 --> 00:45:40.775
We talked about it, a couple
of points I want to mention.

00:45:40.775 --> 00:45:43.520
So you have to stress test
your existing database--

00:45:43.520 --> 00:45:45.890
sorry, your new
Cloud SQL database

00:45:45.890 --> 00:45:48.470
and see that it meets your
performance characteristics.

00:45:48.470 --> 00:45:51.230
You need to make sure that your
application workloads, SLAs

00:45:51.230 --> 00:45:54.530
in terms of query performance
and query responsiveness

00:45:54.530 --> 00:45:55.460
are met.

00:45:55.460 --> 00:45:57.290
We talked about it, essentially.

00:45:57.290 --> 00:46:00.960
But that's stuff you'll have
to do only after you migrated

00:46:00.960 --> 00:46:04.250
the data, so only after you have
like a working copy of Cloud

00:46:04.250 --> 00:46:06.890
SQL Postgres, for
example, with your data,

00:46:06.890 --> 00:46:09.740
and the Compute Engine servers
for running your applications.

00:46:09.740 --> 00:46:12.628
That's when you can
test your applications.

00:46:12.628 --> 00:46:14.420
So that's, again,
something you have to do.

00:46:14.420 --> 00:46:15.920
And also, keep in
mind that it might

00:46:15.920 --> 00:46:18.810
be worthwhile to create a
reverse replication flow.

00:46:18.810 --> 00:46:21.050
So in case you have to do
a rollback-- so let's say

00:46:21.050 --> 00:46:22.130
you planned everything.

00:46:22.130 --> 00:46:23.090
You execute everything.

00:46:23.090 --> 00:46:25.130
Everything is great.

00:46:25.130 --> 00:46:27.020
But then, after
you went live, you

00:46:27.020 --> 00:46:30.140
found out an issue,
maybe an application

00:46:30.140 --> 00:46:32.220
you did end up forgetting.

00:46:32.220 --> 00:46:34.550
So you'll need to kind
of think about creating

00:46:34.550 --> 00:46:37.190
a reverse replication
flow, just in case

00:46:37.190 --> 00:46:39.140
you want to roll
back your changes

00:46:39.140 --> 00:46:41.780
and move back to the
original Oracle database

00:46:41.780 --> 00:46:43.730
until you work things
out, because otherwise,

00:46:43.730 --> 00:46:45.772
if you just set up a
one-way replication process,

00:46:45.772 --> 00:46:47.930
and you move to your
new Postgres target,

00:46:47.930 --> 00:46:50.600
and you start working with the
changes applied to Postgres

00:46:50.600 --> 00:46:52.415
would not be replicated
back to Oracle.

00:46:52.415 --> 00:46:55.840
And it will essentially
be lost when you switch--

00:46:55.840 --> 00:46:57.165
if you need to switch back.

00:46:57.165 --> 00:46:58.290
Let's hope it won't happen.

00:46:58.290 --> 00:47:00.600
But sometimes, it might happen.

00:47:00.600 --> 00:47:02.990
So again, we talked
about all of that.

00:47:02.990 --> 00:47:03.975
It's in the deck.

00:47:03.975 --> 00:47:05.100
I don't have a lot of time.

00:47:05.100 --> 00:47:06.700
So I'm going to go
briefly over that.

00:47:06.700 --> 00:47:07.950
But again, we talked about it.

00:47:07.950 --> 00:47:09.600
And it's in the
deck for reference.

00:47:09.600 --> 00:47:10.700
So do application QA.

00:47:10.700 --> 00:47:13.520
Make your application
behaves as expected, right?

00:47:13.520 --> 00:47:15.300
Nothing breaks, and
performance is good.

00:47:15.300 --> 00:47:18.688
Do query QA and database QA,
both on the workload side,

00:47:18.688 --> 00:47:20.480
but also making sure
that you can failover,

00:47:20.480 --> 00:47:22.258
that you can back up
and restore, the time

00:47:22.258 --> 00:47:23.550
it takes to backup and restore.

00:47:23.550 --> 00:47:27.060
So you'll know your IT's
setup as you need it.

00:47:27.060 --> 00:47:28.310
So you test high availability.

00:47:28.310 --> 00:47:31.010
You test DR. You allocate
time for performance training.

00:47:31.010 --> 00:47:34.448
This is all part of the
migration validation step.

00:47:34.448 --> 00:47:36.740
Compatibility of application--
we talked about it some.

00:47:36.740 --> 00:47:39.980
Modern applications might be
more compatible with Postgres.

00:47:39.980 --> 00:47:43.132
Legacy application might
not be as compatible.

00:47:43.132 --> 00:47:45.590
So it's very important to map
the applications that connect

00:47:45.590 --> 00:47:46.843
to your database right now.

00:47:46.843 --> 00:47:47.510
Are they legacy?

00:47:47.510 --> 00:47:48.080
Are they modern?

00:47:48.080 --> 00:47:50.455
And they using the Oracle Call
Interface, custom drivers,

00:47:50.455 --> 00:47:52.130
JDBC, ODBC?

00:47:52.130 --> 00:47:55.070
Java applications would probably
be easier to port versus,

00:47:55.070 --> 00:47:58.490
I don't know, C++ or
like COBOL applications,

00:47:58.490 --> 00:48:00.320
some of the legacy stuff.

00:48:00.320 --> 00:48:02.630
So you need to make sure
that your applications are

00:48:02.630 --> 00:48:03.370
compatible.

00:48:03.370 --> 00:48:05.120
It's also, sometimes
there is a difference

00:48:05.120 --> 00:48:07.083
between porting
in-house applications

00:48:07.083 --> 00:48:08.250
to third-party applications.

00:48:08.250 --> 00:48:11.055
So if it's an application
you developed in-house,

00:48:11.055 --> 00:48:12.680
you might have the
resources internally

00:48:12.680 --> 00:48:14.960
to make the changes
needed to support

00:48:14.960 --> 00:48:16.940
Postgres or MySQL if needed.

00:48:16.940 --> 00:48:20.450
If it's a third-party
application, maybe--

00:48:20.450 --> 00:48:22.910
and it's not supported, then
that's going to be a problem.

00:48:22.910 --> 00:48:25.970
So map your applications
for compatibility

00:48:25.970 --> 00:48:28.280
and identify specifically
legacy applications

00:48:28.280 --> 00:48:31.190
that would probably
make your migration

00:48:31.190 --> 00:48:34.170
a whole lot more complicated.

00:48:34.170 --> 00:48:35.420
We talked about performance.

00:48:35.420 --> 00:48:37.330
Bottom line-- switching
database engines,

00:48:37.330 --> 00:48:39.080
queries will behave
differently, no matter

00:48:39.080 --> 00:48:40.730
how compatible you
are and how much

00:48:40.730 --> 00:48:42.980
do you think that you're
only using [? NSIS ?] SQL.

00:48:42.980 --> 00:48:47.330
So plan for performance
testing as well.

00:48:47.330 --> 00:48:49.820
We talked about rollback
strategy just a bit, right?

00:48:49.820 --> 00:48:53.570
So create a reverse replication
flow for your data migration,

00:48:53.570 --> 00:48:56.300
making sure changes are applied
back to the source database.

00:48:56.300 --> 00:48:57.290
Sorry.

00:48:57.290 --> 00:48:59.510
And keep your old
environment around

00:48:59.510 --> 00:49:01.427
after you go live with
the new environment.

00:49:01.427 --> 00:49:04.010
Keep the old environment around
for at least a few days, maybe

00:49:04.010 --> 00:49:05.518
a few weeks, just in case.

00:49:05.518 --> 00:49:07.310
Maybe you forgot there
was a script running

00:49:07.310 --> 00:49:08.977
on the Chrome tab of
the Oracle database

00:49:08.977 --> 00:49:10.430
that you forgot about, right?

00:49:10.430 --> 00:49:15.290
So just keep it around,
because you might need it.

00:49:15.290 --> 00:49:17.060
Again, this is
rudimentary stuff.

00:49:17.060 --> 00:49:19.010
But we saw a
project where people

00:49:19.010 --> 00:49:20.660
went live, closed
the old environment,

00:49:20.660 --> 00:49:22.660
and then figured out that
they missed something.

00:49:22.660 --> 00:49:26.230
[MUSIC PLAYING]

